Implementation using json.Decoder:

```go
// rejectDuplicateMembers scans the raw submission and refuses any JSON object
// that lists the same member name twice. Names are compared after JSON string
// decoding (so "inv" and "inv" collide), case-sensitively and
// without trimming. The check applies to every object in the document: the
// top-level object, the artifact, each rule, each check record, and any
// object nested inside unknown fields. String values that merely look like
// JSON are content, not structure, and stay untouched.
func rejectDuplicateMembers(data []byte) error {
	dec := json.NewDecoder(bytes.NewReader(data))
	type frame struct {
		path     string
		keys     map[string]bool // nil for arrays
		isObject bool
		wantKey  bool   // object: next scalar string is a member name
		lastKey  string // object: member name whose value is being read
		count    int    // array: elements seen so far
	}
	var stack []*frame
	// valueDone marks that a value nested in the current top object finished.
	valueDone := func() {
		if len(stack) > 0 && stack[len(stack)-1].isObject {
			stack[len(stack)-1].wantKey = true
		}
	}
	_ = valueDone
	...
}
```

Hmm, let me write it more carefully with a loop:

```go
for {
	tok, err := dec.Token()
	if err == io.EOF {
		return nil
	}
	if err != nil {
		// Malformed JSON is reported by the real decode below.
		return nil
	}
	switch t := tok.(type) {
	case json.Delim:
		switch t {
		case '{':
			stack = append(stack, &frame{path: childPath(...), isObject: true, wantKey: true, keys: map...})
		case '[':
			stack = append(stack, &frame{path: ..., isObject: false})
		case '}', ']':
			stack = stack[:len(stack)-1]
			closeValue()  // mark parent object value complete
		}
	case string:
		top := stack[len(stack)-1] (if any)
		if top != nil && top.isObject && top.wantKey {
			if top.keys[t] { return errInvalid(...) }
			top.keys[t] = true
			top.wantKey = false
			top.lastKey = t
		} else {
			closeValue()
		}
	default: // float64, bool, nil
		closeValue()
	}
}
```

Wait — the top-level value: if the document is just a scalar or array, stack may be empty; handle nil top.

childPath: when pushing a container, its path derives from parent:
- no parent: "$" or "top-level object"
- parent is object: parent.path + "." + parent.lastKey
- parent is array: parent.path + "[" + strconv.Itoa(parent.count) + "]"; increment parent.count.

Also when a scalar value is read inside an array, increment count. So "closeValue" for arrays increments count; for objects sets wantKey=true.

Let me define closeValue():

```go
closeValue := func() {
	if len(stack) == 0 {
		return
	}
	top := stack[len(stack)-1]
	if top.isObject {
		top.wantKey = true
	} else {
		top.count++
	}
}
```

And childPath computed at push time, then call closeValue() after push (the container itself is a value of the parent).

