Note about json.Decoder.Token: by default it doesn't validate that the entire stream is one value? Token reads one token at a time; trailing garbage after top-level value close would be read as another token — e.g. `{} {}` — the second `{` would be pushed as a new root. That's fine; json.Unmarshal will reject trailing data anyway. But duplicate check across two roots — harmless.

